<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Peucetians</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 300 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Iapygian people of central Apulia around modern Bari, whose towns such as Rubi became centres of Greek-influenced art and vase collecting.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Peucetians occupied central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/daunians">Daunians</a> to the north and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/messapians">Messapians</a> to the south, from the early 1st millennium BCE. Greek writers explained their name through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peucetius">Peucetius</a>, a son of the Arcadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycaon-of-arcadia">Lycaon</a>, but their culture developed from local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> communities related to their Iapygian neighbours.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in separate fortified towns, among them Caelia, Azetium, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruvo-di-puglia">Rubi</a> (Ruvo di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Puglia</a>) and the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-sannace">Monte Sannace</a>, without a known central government. Their geometric painted pottery gave way, from the 5th century BCE, to strong Greek influence: the elite of Rubi buried their dead with Athenian and Apulian red-figure vases, many from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taras">Taras</a>. They grew cereals and olives and traded through coastal landings.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Peucetian towns came under Roman control around the end of the 4th and start of the 3rd centuries BCE during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. They were later absorbed into Roman Apulia and became municipia. Collections of vases from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruvo-di-puglia">Ruvo</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jatta-national-museum">Jatta collection</a>, preserve much of their material culture.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/peucetians.jpg" alt="Peucetians" width="320" height="144"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Femmes_peuc%C3%A8tes_dansant,_fresque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 300 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iapygian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Messapic (Iapygian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Peucetii, Poediculi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-550&amp;focus=peucetians" aria-label="Peucetians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4928 587 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Peucetians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5248 843h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5248 843h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Italy. <a href="/atlas?year=-550&amp;focus=peucetians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

